# Windows 10 capture

The guest is the powered-off QEMU disk `virtual-machines/win10-22h2/disk.qcow2` at the project root that contains this checkout. QEMU opens it with `format=qcow2` and `-snapshot`. Do not write the qcow2. Boot with `-accel tcg`, `-m 8192`, and `-smp 4`. Do not pass `-accel whpx` on a capture boot. The VerOS NT boot uses the same memory and CPU count.

Two captures exist. Both stay under `output/`, which is gitignored. After the run, copy the result files named in [capture-log.mdc](../../../.cursor/rules/capture-log.mdc) into `capture-log/` at the project root. Do not commit that folder.

- `tools/windows-trace` records numeric syscall lines: number, status, and `a1`–`a4`. No names and no ordinal map.
- `differential-tests/syscall-diff` in the parent checkout runs one GPL amd64 `probe.exe` on Windows 10 22H2 x64 and on the amd64 VerOS NT boot. It calls the names in `compat/reference/win10-22h2-syscalls.txt` through `GetProcAddress` and prints `DIFF <name> <status>`, `MISSING`, `CRASH`, or `STARTED`, then `DIFF END OK`. COM1 is `-serial file:` with forward slashes. A FAT sink copy is only a backup.

A Windows differential result is evidence only when that guest log contains `DIFF END OK`. A host run of the probe is not a Windows 10 22H2 result. A serial log or abi plugin JSONL verifies a new symbol or struct layout only under [clean-room.mdc](clean-room.mdc).

`differential-tests/syscall-diff`, `differential-tests/graphics-diff`, `differential-tests/kernel-abi`, and `differential-tests/ndis-abi` reuse `output/<tool>/windows.log` when `differential-tests/probe-stamp/Test-WindowsProbe.ps1` prints `REUSE` for that tool. The stamp is `output/<tool>/windows.probe`. It records the probe source hash and the log hash. A change to that probe's sources requires a new Windows 10 boot. Each runner's `-CheckWindows` reports the same decision.

The parent checkout keeps the probe source and the compare script. This tree keeps the name list and a report of names plus status codes. Neither tree may keep a disk image, an ISO, a trace, a disassembly, a header, a `.lib`, a `.sys`, or an object file.

```text
Right: DIFF NtCreateFile 0x00000000
Wrong: a function body, a Windows ordinal, or a pasted WDK declaration
```
